# C Conventions for GBA

## Naming
- Functions and structs: `UpperCamelCase` (e.g. `BattleScriptCmd_AttackAnimation`)
- Constants and macros: `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` (e.g. `MOVE_TACKLE`, `SPECIES_PIKACHU`)
- Local variables and struct fields: `lowerCamelCase`
- Never hardcode numeric IDs — always use the named constants from `include/constants/`

## Memory Sections
```c
// Fast internal RAM (32KB) — use sparingly for hot code paths
IWRAM_CODE void MyFastFunction(void);

// External work RAM (256KB) — default for most data
// No annotation needed; this is the default
```

## Types
- Use `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `s8`, `s16`, `s32`, `bool32` from `gba/types.h`
- Avoid `int`/`long` without explicit width when the size matters for ROM data layout

## Patterns to Follow
- Task system: long-running processes use `CreateTask` / `DestroyTask` with a callback
- Callback system: menu and overworld states use `SetMainCallback2` / `gMain.callback2`
- String formatting: use `ConvertIntToDecimalStringN`, never `sprintf`
- Assertions/tests: add test cases in `test/` rather than debug prints; see `docs/tutorials/how_to_testing_system.md`

## Things to Avoid
- No `malloc` / `free` — no heap allocator exists
- No `printf`, `puts`, or any stdio — no OS
- No recursion in battle or overworld code — fixed call stack size
- Do not add `static` to functions that are referenced from data tables or other translation units
